From b13e2a0d54e317ba1b7977958a13f888ab4f3a66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 18:48:50 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 064/206] feat(shorebird): the IL pass that makes DD calls
 indirect

DD core's other half. Their pass leaves no symbol -- it is inlined away
completely -- so this is reconstructed from what it does to their output
rather than from their code. Running their gen_snapshot over one kernel
twice, with and without --dd_slot_mapping (dd_pipeline.sh, out_dd), pins
every part of it:

  * The mapping is the gate. Without it, zero loads of Thread+0x978; with
    it, three. Same gate as FinalizeIndirectStaticCallTable.
  * The rule is total. patch.so makes three direct calls to DD-slot
    targets (2x Expando.[], 1x _FinalizerImpl._runFinalizers); patch_dd.so
    makes none and has exactly three indirect sites. The five resolved
    slots without call sites produced none.
  * Nothing else moves. A normalised diff of _NativeFinalizer._removeEntries
    across the two builds is one `bl <Expando.[]>` against the three
    instruction indirect sequence, and 76 identical instructions.

That third point also corrects what the dd_resolution `rewritten` column
means. It is per *slot* -- slots 3 and 4 report 1 each -- not per call
site, of which there are three. It was previously recorded as a
counting-level judge for this pass; it is a coarser one than that.

The emitted sequence is transcribed from those sites and is a word shorter
than what was here before, which computed the slot address and called
through it:

    ldr x16, [x26, #0x978]   ; table base out of Thread
    ldr x16, [x16, #0x20]    ; table[dd_slot], slot folded into the immediate
    blr x16

The second load is also what the table *is*: the loaded word is branched
to directly, so entries are bare entry points. A tagged Array of Code
would need the data offset and an untag, and there is neither.

Verified against our own gen_snapshot on their fixture. No mapping: 0
indirect sites. A mapping over all 1272 usable functions: 1724 sites,
across 539 distinct slots. Single-function mappings land exactly:

    kernel_offset 215094 -> 103 sites, 103 direct calls lost, one name
                            (_StringBase._interpolate)
    kernel_offset  18339 ->  79 / 79   (RangeError.checkValidRange)
    kernel_offset  54623 ->  73 / 73   (StringBuffer.write)

Nothing but the mapped function is ever touched. Our sequence differs
from theirs in two immediate fields only -- the Thread offset (ours is
appended at 0xf78) and the slot index -- and `blr x16` is byte-identical.

81 of 81 shorebird tests pass; verify_reproduction.sh is clean at 24
artifacts and 14 stubs, so the ct/dt/ddid reproductions did not move.

Still unverified: how the table reaches Thread::indirect_static_call_table_
at runtime, and whether any of this executes on a device.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

